'''Henri Boris Kagan''' ({{IPA|fr|ɑ̃ʁi kaɡɑ̃|lang}}; born 15 December 1930) is a French chemist. He is currently an [[emeritus professor]] at the [[Paris-Saclay University|Université Paris-Saclay]] in [[France]]. He is widely recognized as a pioneer in the field of [[asymmetric catalysis]]. His discoveries have had far-reaching impacts on the [[pharmaceutical industry]].<ref>{{Cite book | url=https://books.google.com/books?id=XPUZAAAAIAAJ&q=%22Kagan,+henri%22+1930 |title = Who's who in Western Europe|isbn = 978-0-900332-61-6|year = 1981| publisher=International Biographical Centre }}</ref> 

In 2026, he was jointly awarded the [[Nobel Prize in Chemistry]] with [[Kensō Soai]] "for the discovery of non-linear effects and [[autocatalysis]] in asymmetric organic synthesis."<ref name=Nobel>{{Cite web |title=Nobel Prize in Chemistry 2026 |url=https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2026/press-release/ |access-date=7 October 2026 |website=NobelPrize.org |language=en-US |publisher=[[Nobel Foundation]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261007095246/https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2026/press-release/ |archive-date=7 October 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

{{Short description|Literary genre}}
[[File:Boethius imprisoned Consolation of philosophy 1385.jpg|thumb|The Roman philosopher [[Boethius]] wrote ''[[The Consolation of Philosophy]]'' in 524 AD (image from a 1385 manuscript) while imprisoned.]]
'''Prison literature''' is the [[literary genre]] of works written by an author in unwilling confinement, such as a [[prison]], jail or [[house arrest]].<ref name=nytbr>Tony Perrottet. "Serving the Sentence", ''[[New York Times Book Review]]'', July 24, 2011.</ref> The writing can be about prison, informed by it, or simply incidentally written while in prison. It could be a memoir, nonfiction, or fiction.

{{Short description|Natural sciences as described by Aristotle}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=January 2023}}

'''Aristotelian physics''' is the form of [[natural philosophy]] described in the works of the [[Ancient Greek philosophy|Greek philosopher]] [[Aristotle]] (384–322 BC). In his work ''[[Physics (Aristotle)|Physics]]'', Aristotle intended to establish general principles of change that govern all natural bodies, both living and inanimate, celestial and terrestrial{{spaced ndash}}including all motion (change with respect to place), quantitative change (change with respect to size or number), qualitative change, and substantial change ("[[Becoming (philosophy)|coming to be]]" [coming into [[existence]], 'generation'] or "passing away" [no longer existing, 'corruption']).<ref>{{Cite book |last=Pfeiffer |first=Christian |title=Aristotle's Theory of Bodies |date=26 July 2018 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=9780198779728 |pages=12-25}}</ref> To Aristotle, 'physics' was a broad field including subjects which would now be called the [[philosophy of mind]], [[sensory experience]], [[memory]], [[anatomy]] and [[biology]]. It constitutes the foundation of the thought underlying [[Works of Aristotle#Aristotle's works by Bekker numbers|many of his works]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Pfeiffer |first=Christian |title=Aristotle's Theory of Bodies |date=26 July 2018 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=9780198779728 |pages=1-4}}</ref>

Key concepts of Aristotelian physics include the structuring of the [[cosmos]] into concentric spheres, with the [[Earth]] at the centre and [[celestial spheres]] around it. The [[Sublunary sphere|terrestrial sphere]] was made of [[Classical element|four elements]], namely earth, air, fire, and water, subject to change and decay. The celestial spheres were made of a fifth element, an unchangeable [[Aether (classical element)|aether]]. Objects made of these elements have natural motions: those of earth and water tend to fall; those of air and fire, to rise. The speed of such motion depends on their weights and the density of the medium. Aristotle argued that a vacuum could not exist as speeds would become infinite.

Aristotle described [[four causes]] or explanations of change as seen on earth: the material, formal, efficient, and final causes of things.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Reece |first=Bryan C. |date=2019 |title=Aristotle's Four Causes of Action |url=https://doi.org/10.1080/00048402.2018.1482932 |journal=Australasian Journal of Philosophy |volume=97 |issue=2 |pages=213-214}}</ref> As regards living things, [[Aristotle's biology]] relied on observation of what he considered to be ‘natural kinds’, both those he considered basic and the groups to which he considered these belonged. He did not conduct [[experiment]]s in the modern sense, but relied on amassing data, observational procedures such as [[dissection]], and making hypotheses about relationships between measurable quantities such as body size and lifespan.

