{{History of art sidebar}}

'''Western painting''' represents a [[History of art|historical]] and [[Contemporary art|contemporary]] [[Art of Europe|Western]] [[painting]] tradition, from [[classical antiquity|antiquity]] until the present time.<ref>Cole, Bruce ''Art of the Western World: From Ancient Greece to Post Modernism''. Simon and Schuster, 1981, [http://www.simonsays.com/content/book.cfm?tab=25&pid=403665 Simonsays.com] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200407043458/https://vimeo.com/simonandschuster/vod_pages |date=7 April 2020 }} accessed 27 October 2007</ref> 

Initially serving imperial, private, civic, and religious patronage, Western painting later found audiences in the [[aristocracy]] and middle class. From the [[Middle Ages]] through the [[Renaissance]], painters worked for the church and a wealthy aristocracy. The Renaissance saw painting advance significantly, in conjunction with the refinement of drawing, [[perspective (graphical)|perspective]], ambitious [[architecture]], [[tapestry]], [[stained glass]], [[sculpture]], and advent of the [[printing press]]. 

Beginning with the [[Baroque]] era, artists received private commissions from a more educated and prosperous middle class. "[[Art for art's sake]]" started finding expression in the [[Romanticism|Romantic]] painters, influencing [[Impressionism]]. 

Western painting was primarily concerned with [[Representational art|representational]] and traditional art until the mid-19th century, when more [[Modern art|modern]], [[Abstract art|abstract]] and [[Conceptual art|conceptual]] forms gained favor. Commercial [[art museum|museums]] and [[art gallery|galleries]] also became established in the [[art world]] and continued providing patronage into the [[20th-century Western painting|20th century]] and contemporary era.
